#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055813} :activity/id #uuid "8b77adb6-8a92-53e7-92ed-d36c7bd33c6f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050532 :topic/id #uuid "51200c28-f8ae-53dc-9ad8-e5eeb622d5ab" :topic/math-academy-id 67} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056030 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x = 8\n$$\n\nThis equation has a **fractional coefficient** of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nmeans that $x$ is being multiplied by $2$ and divided by $3$.\n\nWe can solve this problem by applying the multiplication principle, twice. First, we multiply both sides by ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{3}x &= 8 \\\\[5pt] {\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\dfrac{2}{3}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\\[5pt] \\cancel{\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x &={\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot 8 \\\\ 2x &=24 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by ${\\color{red}{2}}$ to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x &=24 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{2x}{{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{\\color{red}{2}}} &=\\dfrac{24}{{\\color{red}{2}}} \\\\ x &=12 \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8576ba10-7bde-590e-9219-705766ef6f6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0680c574-1075-5054-8ccb-7b30dd4ebd5e" :step/math-academy-id 53334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054418 :question/id #uuid "2d0477ef-8561-5404-b97a-99a49031c4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-13031"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ee8b693-8158-55a9-adcd-0ece37ad7226" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055422}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Linear Equations Without the Reciprocal"} :step/id #uuid "f2722850-19d7-5950-a993-73bfca094cfc" :step/math-academy-id 53335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056031 :tutorial/content "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6\n$$\n\nwe first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\n, and its reciprocal is\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}\n$$\n\nBecause the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1$, we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\\\[5pt] \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5}}_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\\\[5pt] 1 x &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\\\[5pt] x &= 15 \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "278b0f64-5aa2-525c-b86d-91239623e85b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8151 :tutorial/title "Using the Reciprocal"} :step/id #uuid "5eb35e56-a1bd-56c1-a714-81a02c459c4a" :step/math-academy-id 53338 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054419 :question/id #uuid "9acb3bf7-12c7-51db-a8a6-30111c3b7e86" :question/math-academy-id "e-13032"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08568384-d6f2-503a-98f7-fa3f5052caa1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055363}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Linear Equations With the Reciprocal"} :step/id #uuid "df64d848-3438-5a3f-b29d-6c0a12d59468" :step/math-academy-id 53339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055817}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054420 :question/id #uuid "64b6a66a-c061-52e2-a3aa-e54b7542715c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13033"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc82dc4-a6bc-5677-ac4a-c1cddf29ab58" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055180}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Linear Equations With the Reciprocal"} :step/id #uuid "81196fc6-1741-5940-b813-e17306497c17" :step/math-academy-id 53345}] :activity/title "Solving Linear Equations With Fractional Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054919}]}
